GTKThemeTest 0.18 - riscos-gtk theme test

Every common GTK 3 widget in the RISCOS theme, in one window, through
riscos-gtk's RISC OS backend. Everything you do is shown in the status
line at the bottom and written to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.GTKThemeTest.Log.

Pages
  Buttons and fields   action, shaded, toggle and linked buttons; a link;
                       writable, empty, password and shaded fields; a
                       display field; a number range (adjuster arrows);
                       a pop-up; a writable with a menu; styled text.
  Options and sliders  option and radio icons (on, off, mixed, shaded);
                       a switch; sliders, progress and level bars.
  Lists and text       a long list and a tree with scroll bars; a text
                       view with both scroll bars.
  Dialogues and menus  a message dialogue; a popover; Save as... and
                       Open... (Save and Open boxes); a box to drop files
                       on; a context menu (click Menu in the box); info
                       bars; a calendar.
Menu anywhere opens the program's menu (File, Edit, View with ticks and
a Zoom submenu, Help); the Cancel / Apply buttons (Apply is the default
action button) are on every page. Hover for interactive help (tooltips).

Please try everything, then send back the Log, a photo or screenshot of
each page and of an open menu, and a note of anything that looks or
behaves differently from RISC OS.

New in 0.18: Save boxes and files. On the Dialogues and menus page:
- Save as... opens a RISC OS Save box. Drag its file icon to a Filer
  window: GTKThemeTest saves a small CSV file (Accounts/csv) there, with
  its file type. Or drop it on another program (e.g. Edit) to load it
  there. Typing a full name (ADFS::HD4.$.Accounts/csv) and OK also saves.
- Open... opens an Open box: drag a file to it from a Filer window.
- Drag any file from a Filer window onto "Drop a file here": its name
  shows there.
The status line and the Log say which file was chosen or dropped.

New in 0.17: "All sheets (mixed)" changes when clicked (on, then off).

New in 0.16: follows RISC OS 5's table of window border sprites (what
0.15's Log showed), for the scroll bars.

New in 0.15: finds the window border sprites in the form RISC OS 5 keeps
them, so scroll bars should now match your desktop's.

New in 0.14: Wimp colours read as true colours (white was very light
grey); the Wimp's sprites looked for in its sprite pool directly
(Wimp_BaseOfSprites), and more detail in the Log with RISCOS_GTK_DEBUG.

New in 0.13: the Log's first lines say what the theme took from the
desktop ("riscos-gtk: theme: ..."): Wimp colours, how many Wimp and window
border sprites it found, and where it put them.

New in 0.12: scroll bars are drawn with your desktop's own window border
sprites (*ToolSprites: arrows, wells and sliders, pressed ones too), so
they match the scroll bars of every other window.

New in 0.11: the theme takes your desktop's own look. The colours are
your Wimp colours (so a changed desktop palette shows here too), and the
option icons, radio icons, pop-up menu button and number range arrows are
the Wimp's own sprites, from whatever sprite set your desktop has loaded.
(The "mixed" option icon, which RISC OS hasn't got, is still the theme's.)
*Set RISCOS_GTK_WIMP_THEME 0 before running shows the theme as shipped.

New in 0.10: the scroll wheel scrolls lists, text and anything else that
scrolls (Shift + wheel scrolls sideways). Which window it scrolls follows
your *WimpScroll setting (under the pointer, or the one with the caret).

New in 0.9: pointers in the desktop's pointer colours (the same colours
as the normal arrow pointer); over text, the Wimp's own text pointer
(ptr_write). The text caret is the RISC OS caret: red, with a short bar
across its top and bottom.

New in 0.8: pointer shapes. Over a text field the pointer is an I-beam,
over a link a hand; resize, cross-hair and move pointers where GTK uses
them. Off the window (or over a menu) it's the normal pointer again.

New in 0.7: dragging text no longer shows a black rectangle, and the
drop always lands where the pointer is (the rectangle was GTK's drag
picture, opened as a window under the pointer, and it sometimes took the
drop itself).

New in 0.6: text fields and the clipboard.
- Menu on a text field gives its Cut / Copy / Paste / Delete / Select All
  menu (before 0.6 it didn't open).
- Copy and Cut put the text on the RISC OS clipboard, so Paste works in
  other programs (Edit, StrongED...), and Paste here takes what another
  program copied. RISC OS text is Latin-1: characters outside it become ?.
- Select some text and drag it with Select: into another field here (a
  copy), onto another program's window (it's saved there, as if dragged
  from a Save box), or onto a Filer window (saved as a text file called
  TextFile). The Wimp shows a dashed box while you drag.
- Text dragged into this window from another program, or a text file
  dropped from the Filer, goes into the field under the pointer.
- Adjust in a text field no longer pastes (an X Window habit).
*Set RISCOS_GTK_DEBUG 1 before running also logs the clipboard and
drag and drop messages.

New in 0.5: menus the RISC OS way. There is no menu bar in the window:
click Menu (the middle button) anywhere in the window for the program's
menu, with File, Edit, View and Help as submenus. Things with a menu of
their own keep it: the box on the Dialogues page, and text fields (Cut,
Copy, Paste...). *Set RISCOS_GTK_MENUBAR 1 before running brings GTK's
menu bar back.

New in 0.4: the tab area no longer jumps when Apply (the default
button) is clicked and takes the focus.

New in 0.3: menu text no longer garbled when a menu bar menu first
opens; after releasing the mouse over a menu (or anywhere outside the
window) the next click goes where it should (before, it reopened the
menu bar menu). For a menu trace in the Log: *Set RISCOS_GTK_MENU_DEBUG 1
before running; *Set RISCOS_GTK_DEBUG 1 also logs mouse buttons.

New in 0.2: menus are real Wimp menus. The menu bar's menus open below
their titles, pop-ups open to the right of the field, the context menu
opens at the pointer; keyboard short-cuts are shown in a column, ticks
and shaded entries as usual, submenus (View > Zoom) open from the
arrow. A click elsewhere or Escape closes a menu; choosing with Adjust
keeps it open (try View > Show grid with Adjust).

Known, not done yet:
- Drag and drop of files (Save boxes, loading files other than text) is
  next.
- Insert Emoji (in a text field's menu) isn't made for RISC OS yet.
- Menus have no keyboard navigation of the menu bar itself (F10).

Needs SharedUnixLibrary 1.16+ and ARMEABISupport (PackMan), and a 32bpp
(16 million colour) desktop with RISC OS 5.21 or later.

